And clothes have dialects. Boots can say I work in the mud, I grew up around horses, or I like how these look with jeans. The same pair can say all three, depending on who’s looking and where you’re standing.

A uniform is especially interesting because someone else can start the sentence for you. You walk into the room and people already have a name for you. Then you open your mouth, and what you say can rhyme with what you’re wearing or change how they read it.

That makes the flag braid a garment with a peculiar grammar. Each flag speaks on its own. The braid decides whose colors you see first, which ones appear together, and which ones you have to turn the cloth to find.